course: dictionary
lesson: 12177
title: Words that prove things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words tell you how sure we can be about something. Conclusive is one of them. When a result is conclusive, it proves something so completely that nobody can doubt it. «The footprints in the mud were conclusive proof that the thief had been in the shed.» The footprints leave no doubt, so conclusive means proving something with no doubt left.
explain: Scientists use this word a lot. «One test was not conclusive, so the scientists repeated the experiment.» One test might be a fluke, so they test again. Only when the results match every time is the answer conclusive.
explain: You can also use conclusive to describe a final, clear result. «Her win was conclusive: she beat every other runner by ten seconds.» There was no close finish and no need for a second race. A word's meaning often shows in what the sentence leaves out: here, there is no doubt about who won.
fact: Conclusive means proving something completely, so there is no doubt left.
fact: «Conclusive evidence» is evidence that settles the question.
fact: «The results conclusively showed that the plant needs light to grow.» Conclusively is the adverb: it means in a way that proves it.
fact: In science, one test is rarely conclusive, so scientists repeat their experiments.
fact: Conclusive is a word from the same family as conclude, which means to reach a final decision.
exercise: «The footprints in the mud were conclusive proof that the thief had been in the shed.» What does conclusive mean here? | Small and hard to see | Very muddy | Repeated many times | *Proving something with no doubt left
exercise: A scientist says one test is rarely conclusive, so she repeats it. Why? | Because one test is always wrong | Because repeating is always more fun | *So the result is certain and not just luck | To use up more paper
exercise: Which word means proving something so there is no doubt left? | cautious | *conclusive | curious | careless
test: «Her win was conclusive: she beat every other runner by ten seconds.» What does conclusive suggest here? | A race that was cancelled | A slow, uncertain finish | *A clear, final result with no doubt | A tie between two runners
test: «The results conclusively showed that the plant needs light to grow.» What does conclusively mean? | Very quickly and loudly | Only sometimes | In a quiet, shy way | *In a way that proves it with no doubt
exam: Which sentence uses conclusive correctly? | *The photo was conclusive proof that Sam was at the park. | Conclusive is the colour of the photo. | The photo was conclusive, so Sam ran away. | The photo was conclusive and very hungry.
